---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import ComplexityBadge from "../../components/ComplexityBadge.astro";
import GCounterDemo from "../../components/GCounterDemo.astro";
import SharedCounterDemo from "../../components/SharedCounterDemo.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return ["g-counter", "shared-counter"].map((counter) => ({
    params: { counter },
    props: { counter },
  }));
}

interface Props { counter: string }
const { counter } = Astro.props;
const growOnly = counter === "g-counter";
const name = growOnly ? "G-counter" : "SharedCounter";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${name} live lab`}
  description={`Run the ${name} across three tabs or devices with a real room service and Watershed kernels.`}
>
  <article class="live-counter-lab">
    <header class="lab-intro">
      <a href={`/structures/${counter}/`}>Return to the {name} lesson</a>
      <h1>{name} live lab</h1>
      <ComplexityBadge complexity="intermediate" />
      <p>Open one room across three tabs or devices. You control one hiker and compare the local notebooks as you exchange updates.</p>
    </header>

    {growOnly ? <GCounterDemo live /> : <SharedCounterDemo live />}

    <section class="lab-experiment" aria-labelledby="experiment-title">
      <h2 id="experiment-title">Try a concurrent update</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Create a room. Open its link in two more tabs, or share the link with two other devices. Check that Alice, Bob, and Carol have connected.</li>
        <li>{growOnly
          ? "In Alice's tab, record +7. In Bob's tab, record +3. Make both changes before comparing the notebooks."
          : "In Alice's tab, send +3. In Bob's tab, send -1. Make both changes before comparing the notebooks."}</li>
        <li>{growOnly
          ? "From a reset count of 0, all copies should reach 10. Leave the room and join again to load the stored counts."
          : "From a reset count of 10, all copies should reach 12. Compare the numbered log, then leave and rejoin to replay it."}</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Reset clears the shared room for all connected hikers. An observer can read the room but cannot edit or reset it.</p>
    </section>

    <details class="lab-notes">
      <summary>How the real connection and storage work</summary>
      <section aria-labelledby="live-room-title">
        <h2 id="live-room-title">{growOnly ? "Stored counts and local copies" : "The real sequencer and storage"}</h2>
        <p>
          Each browser runs three Watershed kernel states. The server assigns
          Alice, Bob, and Carol to the first three connections. Further connections
          can observe. You can send changes only for your assigned hiker.
          Roles belong to connections, not authenticated identities.
        </p>
        <p>
          One Cloudflare <dfn><a href="/glossary/#durable-object">Durable Object</a></dfn>
          {" "}provides the room service and its private SQLite storage.
          A <dfn><a href="/glossary/#websocket">WebSocket</a></dfn> keeps your
          browser connected so the server can send updates.
        </p>
        <TermCallout
          term="Durable Object"
          definition="A Cloudflare service that combines coordination for one named object with storage private to that object."
          inlineOnly
        />
        <TermCallout
          term="WebSocket"
          definition="A persistent connection over which a browser and a server can send messages to each other."
          inlineOnly
        />
        {growOnly ? (
          <Fragment>
            <p>
              Your browser applies a new count before the server replies. It
              sends your hiker's cumulative component count. The server stores
              the largest count for each hiker and broadcasts the state.
              Your browser merges that state through the G-counter kernel.
              Repeated or older state does not add the same birds twice.
            </p>
            <p>
              Rejoining loads the stored three component counts. Reset
              clears them to zero and creates a new <dfn><a href="/glossary/#epoch">epoch</a></dfn>. The server rejects
              changes from the old epoch.
            </p>
            <p><a href="/atlas/multi-device-g-counter/">How multi-device rooms and storage work</a></p>
          </Fragment>
        ) : (
          <Fragment>
            <p>
              When Alice clicks <code>+3</code>, her browser calls the kernel's
              increment operation and sends the signed amount with a unique
              action ID. The server assigns the next sequence number and stores
              the operation before broadcasting it. Each operation contains
              its action ID, author, signed amount, and sequence number.
              The server does not broadcast cumulative counter values.
            </p>
            <p>
              Alice's browser matches its pending edit with the numbered echo
              and calls the kernel's acknowledgement operation. Her count does
              not increase twice. Other copies apply the amount through the
              kernel's remote-operation path. The browser ignores repeated
              sequence numbers. If a later number arrives first, it holds that
              operation and requests the missing prefix.
            </p>
            <p>
              SQLite retains up to 1,000 operations per room. Rejoining starts
              from the agreed count of 10 and replays the stored log in order.
              Reset clears the log, restores 10, and creates a new <dfn><a href="/glossary/#epoch">epoch</a></dfn>.
              The server rejects requests from the old epoch. After a rejected
              edit, the browser rolls back pending edits and reloads the log.
            </p>
          </Fragment>
        )}
        <TermCallout
          term="epoch"
          definition="An identifier for one generation of a room's counter state. Reset creates a new epoch."
          inlineOnly
        />
        <p>
          The optional Speed, Jitter, and Broadcast controls affect delivery
          among the local copies in this browser. They do not delay the real
          server's storage or message exchange. The center log shows the
          operations in this local model; it is not a network packet trace.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no persistent offline outbox or automatic reconnect.
          If the connection closes, you cannot make further edits. Values may
          include unconfirmed changes. Rejoin to recover
          {growOnly ? " the stored counts." : " the stored log."}
        </p>
      </section>
    </details>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lab-intro > a { display: inline-flex; min-height: 44px; align-items: center; }
  .lab-intro h1 { margin-block: var(--space-3); }
  .lab-intro > p { margin-block: var(--space-4) 0; }
  .lab-experiment { max-width: var(--measure-reading); margin-block: var(--space-6); }
  .lab-experiment li { padding-block: var(--space-2); }
  .lab-notes { border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line); }
  .lab-notes summary { min-height: 44px; padding-block: var(--space-4); font-family: var(--font-ui); font-weight: 650; cursor: pointer; }
  .lab-notes section { padding-bottom: var(--space-4); }
</style>
